  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-essay (with a wry, Terminator-framed tone) arguing that the real agentic-AI threat isn't a single breakthrough but the multiplication of two ordinary compounding curves: agent *endurance* (METR's task-length doubling every ~4–7 months) and *breadth* (orchestrators coordinating dozens of sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic's GTG-1002 espionage disruption). The recurring metaphor maps the Terminator franchise onto AI risk: the killer robot is \"an API call,\" and \"absolutely will not stop\" becomes \"absolutely will not stop billing.\" A key economic argument is \"LLMjacking\" (Sysdig): attackers using stolen cloud credentials mean capability gates fail because the victim, not the attacker, pays the meter. Finally, it argues swarm economics defeat defenders' per-attempt-failure reasoning, since cheap automated attempts make aggregate success near-certain (1 − (1−p)^n). Intended audience: engineering/security teams skeptical of agentic-AI hype.",
